WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:01.110
Sir Winston Churchill: The Indispensable Lion

00:00:01.110 --> 00:00:04.840
We imagine Winston Churchill with his signature
cane, drinking scotch whiskey, and puffing

00:00:04.840 --> 00:00:06.840
on a Cuban cigar.

00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:10.620
His mouth is downturned, and his voice is
gruff and his words pointed.

00:00:10.620 --> 00:00:15.690
This is the image Hollywood portrays but it
is a mere caricature of the flesh and blood

00:00:15.690 --> 00:00:16.690
version.

00:00:16.690 --> 00:00:18.300
Who was Winston Churchill?

00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:23.040
In Britain’s “darkest hour,” Churchill
led his country from the brink of Nazi conquest

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:25.540
by forging an alliance with the U.S. and Russia.

00:00:25.540 --> 00:00:28.160
He had many critics, and made mistakes on
a grand scale.

00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:32.340
Yet, above it all, possessed an unwavering
belief in his own power.

00:00:32.340 --> 00:00:36.900
To his beloved country he offered his “blood,
toil, tears and sweat.”

00:00:36.900 --> 00:00:41.060
Behind the political figure was a family man,
a man of literary distinction, an artist,

00:00:41.060 --> 00:00:45.239
a great lover of pets, and a tinker -- he
had many hobbies.

00:00:45.239 --> 00:00:52.700
Today on Biographics we paint the complex
picture of “The Lion,” Sir Winston Churchill.

00:00:52.700 --> 00:00:59.399
Formative Years

00:00:59.399 --> 00:01:04.110
Sir Winston Churchill was born on November
30, 1874 into the upper echelons of British

00:01:04.110 --> 00:01:05.110
society.

00:01:05.110 --> 00:01:09.790
His father was Lord Randolph Henry Spencer-Churchill,
a prominent politician and the third son of

00:01:09.790 --> 00:01:11.490
the of the seventh Duke of Marlborough.

00:01:11.490 --> 00:01:15.070
Churchill’s mother, Jennie Jerome, was an
American beauty and socialite, the daughter

00:01:15.070 --> 00:01:18.100
of a wealthy New York stock broker and financier.

00:01:18.100 --> 00:01:22.480
The glamorous couple would seem like a match
made in heaven but they lived mostly apart

00:01:22.480 --> 00:01:23.950
and carried on separate lives.

00:01:23.950 --> 00:01:27.869
They also couldn’t be bothered with the
young Winston; the very privilege of his pedigree

00:01:27.869 --> 00:01:31.880
didn’t afford him the kind of love and affection
he sought from his parents.

00:01:31.880 --> 00:01:37.180
Sadly, throughout his life, both father and
mother were distant and neglectful.

00:01:37.180 --> 00:01:41.640
Fortunately for the young Winston, there was
one adult in his life who provided emotional

00:01:41.640 --> 00:01:46.270
support and acted like a mother figure — Churchill’s
nanny, Elizabeth Everest.

00:01:46.270 --> 00:01:51.200
“Woom,” he called her after a failed attempt
at saying woman as a child, was hired to care

00:01:51.200 --> 00:01:53.560
for Winston when he was one month old.

00:01:53.560 --> 00:01:57.790
She would remain his only real friend and
confidante throughout his childhood.

00:01:57.790 --> 00:02:02.200
When she passed away, Churchill said of her,
"She had been my dearest and most intimate

00:02:02.200 --> 00:02:04.940
friend during the whole twenty years I had
lived.

00:02:04.940 --> 00:02:07.570
I shall never know such a friend again."

00:02:07.570 --> 00:02:11.720
Winston was just seven years old when he was
sent away to St. George’s School at Ascot,

00:02:11.720 --> 00:02:12.860
a boarding school.

00:02:12.860 --> 00:02:16.560
It was not uncommon for schoolchildren in
upper-class society to be sent away, but his

00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:20.170
mother dropped him off at Paddington Station
alone to catch the train.

00:02:20.170 --> 00:02:25.700
At his first school, Winston was bullied and
bruised by the headmaster who commonly “birched”

00:02:25.700 --> 00:02:29.670
the students, a form of corporal punishment
that involved striking the bare bottoms of

00:02:29.670 --> 00:02:30.930
boys that misbehaved.

00:02:30.930 --> 00:02:35.040
With visible scars on Winston from the beatings,
and pleas from his loving nanny, his parents

00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:38.510
withdrew him and placed him at Brighton School
in 1882.

00:02:38.510 --> 00:02:42.590
Winston escaped the brutality of his first
school but not the lessons at Brighton, which

00:02:42.590 --> 00:02:43.659
he despised.

00:02:43.659 --> 00:02:48.390
Winston hated the regiment and was rebellious;
his poor academic scores and speech impediment,

00:02:48.390 --> 00:02:52.520
a lisp, made Winston an easy target and subject
to ridicule.

00:02:52.520 --> 00:02:57.410
In 1888, Winston enrolled at the Harrow School
but with abysmal entry scores, he placed at

00:02:57.410 --> 00:02:58.510
the bottom of his class.

00:02:58.510 --> 00:03:02.760
At Harrow, Winston excelled only in the subjects
that interested him, including English and

00:03:02.760 --> 00:03:03.760
history.

00:03:03.760 --> 00:03:07.760
He demonstrated a prowess for the sport of
fencing, winning the Public Schools’ fencing

00:03:07.760 --> 00:03:08.760
championship.

00:03:08.760 --> 00:03:12.080
Yet, the few bright spots were overshadowed
by deep sadness.

00:03:12.080 --> 00:03:16.281
Winston recalled, “... In retrospect those
years form not only the least agreeable, but

00:03:16.281 --> 00:03:19.709
also the only barren and unhappy period of
my life."

00:03:19.709 --> 00:03:23.990
Winston Churchill nearly died from a self-imposed
act of bravado at the age of 18 when he jumped

00:03:23.990 --> 00:03:25.050
off a bridge.

00:03:25.050 --> 00:03:30.769
He would endure three days of unconsciousness,
a ruptured kidney, and three months in bed.

00:03:30.769 --> 00:03:34.680
It happened when the teenager was playing
chase with his 12-year old brother Jack and

00:03:34.680 --> 00:03:37.519
14-year old cousin while vacationing at their
aunt’s estate.

00:03:37.519 --> 00:03:42.080
According to Churchill in his 1930 memoir,
he was “trapped” on a “rustic bridge”

00:03:42.080 --> 00:03:44.710
with brother at one end and cousin at the
other.

00:03:44.710 --> 00:03:48.600
Rather than be “captured,” Churchill lept
from the bridge, aiming for a pine tree to

00:03:48.600 --> 00:03:49.600
slide down.

00:03:49.600 --> 00:03:54.000
He greatly misjudged the distance and fell
the full height onto the hard ground.

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:58.549
Churchill was extremely lucky to survive but
it wouldn’t be his last brush with death.

00:03:58.549 --> 00:04:02.489
During his life Churchill was prone to accidents,
including crashing a plane while learning

00:04:02.489 --> 00:04:07.410
to fly, nearly drowning in a lake, falling
several times from horses, and getting hit

00:04:07.410 --> 00:04:10.989
by a car while trying to cross New York’s
Fifth Avenue.

00:04:10.989 --> 00:04:13.549
Military Service &amp; War Correspondent

00:04:13.549 --> 00:04:18.049
Due to his poor grades at Harrow, it appeared
Churchill’s only chance at a career was

00:04:18.049 --> 00:04:19.190
through the army.

00:04:19.190 --> 00:04:22.740
It took Churchill three tries to pass the
admittance exam for the Royal Military College

00:04:22.740 --> 00:04:23.810
at Sandhurst.

00:04:23.810 --> 00:04:28.030
Churchill described taking the Latin portion
of the exam and leaving it completely blank

00:04:28.030 --> 00:04:31.689
except for his name, a number, and “a blot
and several smudges.”

00:04:31.689 --> 00:04:35.520
His father had wanted him to be in the Infantry
but his scores were only good enough for the

00:04:35.520 --> 00:04:36.520
Cavalry.

00:04:36.520 --> 00:04:41.050
The new training at Sandhurst excited Churchill
and over the next 18 months Churchill thrived.

00:04:41.050 --> 00:04:46.130
He recalled, "I was no longer handicapped
by past neglect of Latin, French or Mathematics.

00:04:46.130 --> 00:04:49.800
We had now to learn fresh things and we all
started equal.

00:04:49.800 --> 00:04:54.159
Tactics, Fortification, Topography [mapmaking],
Military Law and Military Fortification, formed

00:04:54.159 --> 00:04:55.479
the whole curriculum.

00:04:55.479 --> 00:04:57.830
In addition were Drill, Gymnastics and Riding."

00:04:57.830 --> 00:05:01.520
Churchill finished twentieth out of 130 in
his graduating class.

00:05:01.520 --> 00:05:04.860
Churchill craved action and showed no fear
on the battlefront.

00:05:04.860 --> 00:05:09.439
He knew he was destined for great things and
would prove it to the world...war provided

00:05:09.439 --> 00:05:11.379
the perfect opportunity.

00:05:11.379 --> 00:05:16.300
Churchill served in the British Army for only
a short time, from 1895 to 1899, and during

00:05:16.300 --> 00:05:21.560
the same time wrote military reports for newspapers
and two books, The Story of the Malakand Field

00:05:21.560 --> 00:05:23.439
Force and The River War.

00:05:23.439 --> 00:05:28.340
In 1899, Churchill tried to win election in
the House of Commons and failed.

00:05:28.340 --> 00:05:32.390
Without hesitation, he traveled to South Africa
to cover Britain’s war with the Boers as

00:05:32.390 --> 00:05:34.939
a London Post war correspondent.

00:05:34.939 --> 00:05:39.280
At the age of 25, Churchill arrived in Cape
Town with 18 bottles of scotch whiskey and

00:05:39.280 --> 00:05:43.759
a guarantee of $150,000 in today’s money,
for a four-month assignment.

00:05:43.759 --> 00:05:48.849
One day, Churchill admitted to his brother,
“There is no ambition I cherish so keenly

00:05:48.849 --> 00:05:52.020
as to gain a reputation for personal courage.”

00:05:52.020 --> 00:05:55.059
He would soon get the chance to earn that
reputation in South Africa.

00:05:55.059 --> 00:05:58.960
While covering the war, Churchill was aboard
a train with soldiers sent on a reconnaissance

00:05:58.960 --> 00:05:59.960
mission.

00:05:59.960 --> 00:06:03.979
The train was suddenly ambushed by the enemy
and Churchill immediately sprung into action.

00:06:03.979 --> 00:06:07.960
Under fire, he acted like a decorated general
and ordered the soldiers with him to flee

00:06:07.960 --> 00:06:09.090
to safety.

00:06:09.090 --> 00:06:13.740
Most of them did but Churchill was captured
as a prisoner of war and sent to Pretoria.

00:06:13.740 --> 00:06:16.460
Churchill’s conditions in captivity could
have been worse.

00:06:16.460 --> 00:06:19.780
The Boers allowed prisoners to buy cigarettes,
beer, and newspapers after all.

00:06:19.780 --> 00:06:24.969
But Churchill hated every moment of it, feeling
the opportunity for glory slipping him by.

00:06:24.969 --> 00:06:29.439
One night, with no real plan of how to make
it out of the 300 miles of enemy territory,

00:06:29.439 --> 00:06:31.159
he made a daring escape.

00:06:31.159 --> 00:06:35.830
A massive manhunt ensued and the fugitive
Churchill was wanted “dead or alive.”

00:06:35.830 --> 00:06:39.560
He avoided capture, travelling at night and
drinking water from streams and when he could

00:06:39.560 --> 00:06:43.340
go no further, he took a chance by knocking
on a stranger’s door.

00:06:43.340 --> 00:06:46.349
On the other side, he knew not whether friend
or foe.

00:06:46.349 --> 00:06:49.620
Saved by pure chance or fate, it was an Englishman
who answered.

00:06:49.620 --> 00:06:53.960
“I felt like a drowning man pulled out of
the water,” Churchill would recall.

00:06:53.960 --> 00:06:58.389
The Englishman was John Howard, a coal mine
manager who hid Churchill in a pitch-black

00:06:58.389 --> 00:07:04.270
mine before he could be smuggled to safety...rats
were his only company.

00:07:04.270 --> 00:07:08.009
In Britain, the story of an English lord’s
son escaping prison camp and humiliating the

00:07:08.009 --> 00:07:10.250
Boers was a boost to the nation.

00:07:10.250 --> 00:07:12.810
He became a hero and a celebrity overnight.

00:07:12.810 --> 00:07:15.860
Early Years in Parliament

00:07:15.860 --> 00:07:21.830
With a hero’s welcome and newfound popularity
Churchill ran for office, and this time in

00:07:21.830 --> 00:07:25.429
1900, he was elected as Member of Parliament
in the House of Commons.

00:07:25.429 --> 00:07:33.020
This was the start of a 55-year long political
career with the highest of highs and the lowest

00:07:33.020 --> 00:07:34.020
of lows.

00:07:34.020 --> 00:07:37.899
Churchill once said, "Politics is almost as
exciting as war, and quite as dangerous.

00:07:37.899 --> 00:07:42.029
In war you can only be killed once, but in
politics many times."

00:07:42.029 --> 00:07:45.319
Churchill was a Conservative (Tory) when he
first stepped into the House of Commons but

00:07:45.319 --> 00:07:48.749
he repeatedly clashed with his party on a
number of issues.

00:07:48.749 --> 00:07:53.270
Free Trade was crucial in his mind and in
1904, it was his party’s support of tariffs

00:07:53.270 --> 00:07:56.810
that made Churchill ‘cross the floor,’
to the opposing Liberal side.

00:07:56.810 --> 00:08:00.699
It infuriated his fellow Conservatives but
it proved to be great timing; the Liberals

00:08:00.699 --> 00:08:02.469
took control soon after.

00:08:02.469 --> 00:08:06.650
As a newfound Liberal, Churchill concerned
himself with social reforms and laid the groundwork

00:08:06.650 --> 00:08:08.469
for the post-war ‘welfare state.’

00:08:08.469 --> 00:08:12.300
Churchill again “ratted” -- or switched
sides -- back to the Conservative Party in

00:08:12.300 --> 00:08:13.379
1925.

00:08:13.379 --> 00:08:18.129
The flippant politician was highly criticized
for the moves and he was seen as an untrustworthy

00:08:18.129 --> 00:08:19.129
opportunist.

00:08:19.129 --> 00:08:23.580
Churchill’s greatest political failure,
the one that nearly ruined his career, came

00:08:23.580 --> 00:08:24.929
in 1915.

00:08:24.929 --> 00:08:29.499
World War I was at a stalemate along the Western
Front with Allied troops engaged in deadly

00:08:29.499 --> 00:08:30.739
trench warfare.

00:08:30.739 --> 00:08:35.839
Anxious to end it, Britain’s War Cabinet
authorized a bold military attack, the mastermind

00:08:35.839 --> 00:08:39.870
of the 40-year old First Lord of the Admiralty,
Winston Churchill.

00:08:39.870 --> 00:08:45.050
The campaign would send troops over 1,000
miles to the east, establishing a second front

00:08:45.050 --> 00:08:50.600
to eventually seize Constantinople and take
control of the sea route from Europe to Russia.

00:08:50.600 --> 00:08:54.870
The plan involved a naval attack through the
Dardanelles strait, then a land invasion on

00:08:54.870 --> 00:08:57.110
the Gallopini peninsula in modern day Turkey.

00:08:57.110 --> 00:09:02.360
Lack of intelligence, incompetence, and hesitation
by military commanders created the perfect

00:09:02.360 --> 00:09:05.560
storm and the campaign went horribly wrong.

00:09:05.560 --> 00:09:10.930
Half of the ships were lost and a bloodbath
on the shores lasted nine months.

00:09:10.930 --> 00:09:16.540
In all, 46,000 Allied troops and 65,000 Turkish
lost their lives.

00:09:16.540 --> 00:09:20.980
Right or wrong, Churchill bore the brunt of
the failure and it forced his resignation

00:09:20.980 --> 00:09:25.959
from politics for a time...Churchill then
returned to the warfront.

00:09:25.959 --> 00:09:30.860
The Gallopini campaign haunted Churchill for
decades -- “Remember the Dardanelles,”

00:09:30.860 --> 00:09:34.220
was a favorite taunt from political opponents
throughout his career.

00:09:34.220 --> 00:09:38.889
A second controversy would plague Churchill
for a decade and send him into political exile,

00:09:38.889 --> 00:09:41.360
a period known as the “Wilderness Years.”

00:09:41.360 --> 00:09:46.930
In 1929, a debate erupted over self-government
in India, at the time under British rule.

00:09:46.930 --> 00:09:50.960
Churchill was no doubt an Imperialist and
was vehemently opposed.

00:09:50.960 --> 00:09:55.220
He also had no love for Gandhi; he saw him
as a threat to the British empire and went

00:09:55.220 --> 00:10:00.339
so far as to say, "Gandhi should not be released
on the account of a mere threat of fasting,"

00:10:00.339 --> 00:10:05.180
and "We should be rid of a bad man and an
enemy of the Empire if he died."

00:10:05.180 --> 00:10:10.620
Except for the most right-winged of Conservatives,
Churchill was alone in his position.

00:10:10.620 --> 00:10:13.920
Personal Life

00:10:13.920 --> 00:10:17.970
Much has been written about Winston Churchill
the statesman, but what was ‘the bulldog’

00:10:17.970 --> 00:10:18.970
like at home?

00:10:18.970 --> 00:10:21.250
Who was he to his family and friends?

00:10:21.250 --> 00:10:26.600
When Clementine Hozier met the 29-year old
Winston in 1904 at a dance, she wasn't impressed.

00:10:26.600 --> 00:10:31.610
He didn’t ask her to dance, just stared,
and “never uttered one word and was very

00:10:31.610 --> 00:10:32.610
gauche.”

00:10:32.610 --> 00:10:36.509
Four years later they would meet again at
a dinner party and this time a romance blossomed.

00:10:36.509 --> 00:10:41.189
The couple married in 1908 and would endure
57 years together until Winston’s death

00:10:41.189 --> 00:10:42.949
at the age of 90.

00:10:42.949 --> 00:10:46.879
Even though Clementine’s politics differed
from Winston’s, she was fiercely supportive

00:10:46.879 --> 00:10:50.360
and made standing by her man her number one
priority.

00:10:50.360 --> 00:10:54.730
She quietly advised him and was the only person
who could reprimand his behavior.

00:10:54.730 --> 00:10:58.379
Clementine once said, “she would have loved
to have been a statesman in her own right

00:10:58.379 --> 00:11:01.819
if only she had been born with trousers rather
than petticoats.”

00:11:01.819 --> 00:11:06.300
Winston was overbearing and temperamental
at times but he was always transparent in

00:11:06.300 --> 00:11:11.470
his love for his wife, proudly boasting to
Roosevelt, “I tell Clemmie everything.”

00:11:11.470 --> 00:11:14.840
On occasion, the couple would fight though
-- money was a constant worry for Clementine

00:11:14.840 --> 00:11:18.959
who was born into the aristocracy but wound
up penniless in her youth.

00:11:18.959 --> 00:11:23.170
Winston overspent, enjoyed gambling, and was
in and out of debt.

00:11:23.170 --> 00:11:27.339
During one passionate dispute, Clementine
famously threw a plate of spinach at her husband

00:11:27.339 --> 00:11:28.339
(it missed).

00:11:28.339 --> 00:11:32.600
And, she never quite forgave him for buying
the family home and money pit Chartwell; doing

00:11:32.600 --> 00:11:35.490
so on the sly when she was laid up from giving
birth.

00:11:35.490 --> 00:11:38.839
The couple had five children but only four
would survive into adulthood.

00:11:38.839 --> 00:11:44.839
Their third child, Marigold, died at the age
of two and a half from septicemia (infection

00:11:44.839 --> 00:11:47.560
of the blood) -- a condition modern-day antibiotics
would cure.

00:11:47.560 --> 00:11:53.350
Clementine “shrieked in agony, like an animal
in mortal pain,” upon her daughter’s death.

00:11:53.350 --> 00:11:56.459
One year later, the Churchill’s fourth child,
Mary, was born.

00:11:56.459 --> 00:11:59.220
Winston was delighted to have the nursery
in use again.

00:11:59.220 --> 00:12:03.360
Churchill’s duties as statesman often took
him away from home but he was devoted to his

00:12:03.360 --> 00:12:04.360
family.

00:12:04.360 --> 00:12:08.040
Determined to be different from his own parents
who were distant and cold, whenever he could,

00:12:08.040 --> 00:12:09.129
he dotted on his children.

00:12:09.129 --> 00:12:13.470
He built them a treehouse at the family home,
and used his skills as a bricklayer, to construct

00:12:13.470 --> 00:12:14.800
a little summer house for Mary.

00:12:14.800 --> 00:12:20.580
Over 40 years at Chartwell, Churchill proudly
improved the property by his own hands, building

00:12:20.580 --> 00:12:22.610
ponds, structures, and bridges.

00:12:22.610 --> 00:12:26.089
The Churchill’s had a multitude of pets
-- domestic and exotic.

00:12:26.089 --> 00:12:28.529
Churchill was an animal lover his whole life.

00:12:28.529 --> 00:12:32.499
As a teen in 1891, he even sold his bicycle
to buy a bulldog named “Dodo.”

00:12:32.499 --> 00:12:36.990
At the family home they had both cats and
dogs -- as well as pigs, fish, butterflies,

00:12:36.990 --> 00:12:38.959
sheep, horses, and black swans.

00:12:38.959 --> 00:12:42.220
There was even a parrot called “Charlie”
who it was rumored Churchill taught to squawk

00:12:42.220 --> 00:12:47.899
obscenities against Hitler and the Nazis -- though
this hasn’t been proven and is likely a

00:12:47.899 --> 00:12:48.899
myth.

00:12:48.899 --> 00:12:52.820
When not enamored with politics and acting
as “savior of his country” Churchill was

00:12:52.820 --> 00:12:57.310
writing, painting, playing polo, laying bricks
at the family home, or vacationing with friends

00:12:57.310 --> 00:13:00.620
in Monte Carlo or another exotic location.

00:13:00.620 --> 00:13:03.550
‘The Darkest Hour’

00:13:03.550 --> 00:13:08.040
“We shall fight on the beaches, we shall
fight on the landing grounds, we shall fight

00:13:08.040 --> 00:13:13.699
in the fields and in the streets, we shall
fight in the hills; we shall never surrender.”

00:13:13.699 --> 00:13:18.959
If it wasn’t for Hitler and the Nazis, Churchill
may never have become Britain’s Prime Minister.

00:13:18.959 --> 00:13:23.529
After losing his cabinet position in 1929,
many believed Churchill’s political career

00:13:23.529 --> 00:13:24.629
was over.

00:13:24.629 --> 00:13:28.330
Even though he was out of politics, he was
not out of the public eye.

00:13:28.330 --> 00:13:33.129
He continued to write books, speeches, and
newspaper articles -- and warned of the dangers

00:13:33.129 --> 00:13:34.129
of Hitler.

00:13:34.129 --> 00:13:37.939
From his home in Kent, his predictions of
the growing Nazi power, and quest for domination

00:13:37.939 --> 00:13:39.120
was coming true.

00:13:39.120 --> 00:13:44.339
He later wrote in his memoirs, “My warnings
over the last six years had been so numerous,

00:13:44.339 --> 00:13:49.769
so detailed, and were now so terribly vindicated,
that no one could gainsay me.”

00:13:49.769 --> 00:13:53.329
At the war’s outbreak then Prime Minister
Neville Chamberlain brought Churchill back

00:13:53.329 --> 00:13:57.899
into the fray as Britain’s First Lord of
the Admiralty, the same position he held during

00:13:57.899 --> 00:13:59.250
the first World War.

00:13:59.250 --> 00:14:03.819
Chamberlain had tried, and failed, to strike
a peace deal with Hitler and he showed himself

00:14:03.819 --> 00:14:06.480
incapable of turning the rapid tide of fascism.

00:14:06.480 --> 00:14:09.550
Chamberlain lost the support of his cabinet
when British troops couldn’t prevent the

00:14:09.550 --> 00:14:11.930
occupation of Norway in 1940.

00:14:11.930 --> 00:14:16.200
Chamberlain resigned, and Churchill took over
as Prime Minister at the “darkest hour.”

00:14:16.200 --> 00:14:20.889
The same day, May 10, Hitler invaded Holland,
Belgium, and the Netherlands.

00:14:20.889 --> 00:14:24.029
Churchill was a brilliant orator; thanks to
his mastery of words.

00:14:24.029 --> 00:14:28.170
He inspired his countrymen to fight, and some
of his speeches during the early days of the

00:14:28.170 --> 00:14:31.790
war are some of the most powerful in the English
language.

00:14:31.790 --> 00:14:38.129
Victory was the only option, he never considered
defeat, even when the outcome looked grim.

00:14:38.129 --> 00:14:41.639
When France fell to the Nazis, Britain was
alone in its fight.

00:14:41.639 --> 00:14:46.870
Churchill knew his island nation could not
win the war alone -- he would need the United

00:14:46.870 --> 00:14:47.870
States.

00:14:47.870 --> 00:14:52.509
And, when Germany invaded Russia, Churchill
saw an opportunity to forge an unlikely alliance.

00:14:52.509 --> 00:14:56.680
Britain came to Stalin’s aid and Churchill
defended the decision during the desperate

00:14:56.680 --> 00:15:01.680
time, saying, “No one has been a more consistent
opponent of Communism for the last twenty-five

00:15:01.680 --> 00:15:02.680
years.

00:15:02.680 --> 00:15:04.879
I will unsay no word I have spoken about it.

00:15:04.879 --> 00:15:08.649
But all this fades away before the spectacle
which is now unfolding.

00:15:08.649 --> 00:15:12.870
The past, with its crimes, its follies, its
tragedies, flashes away.…

00:15:12.870 --> 00:15:17.250
The Russian danger is therefore our danger,
and the danger of the United States, just

00:15:17.250 --> 00:15:22.269
as the cause of any Russian fighting for hearth
and house is the cause of free men and free

00:15:22.269 --> 00:15:26.111
peoples in every quarter of the globe.”

00:15:26.111 --> 00:15:29.639
To forge the big three alliance, Churchill
cultivated a friendship with the American

00:15:29.639 --> 00:15:33.980
president, Franklin D. Roosevelt to convince
him to join the war effort.

00:15:33.980 --> 00:15:37.829
He invited himself to the White House and
arrived two weeks after the bombing of Pearl

00:15:37.829 --> 00:15:38.829
Harbor.

00:15:38.829 --> 00:15:42.569
The Prime Minister stayed three weeks, through
Christmas, and well past his welcome in Eleanor

00:15:42.569 --> 00:15:43.569
Roosevelt’s eyes.

00:15:43.569 --> 00:15:48.440
But, it was critical to solidifying their
alliance and winning over the Americans.

00:15:48.440 --> 00:15:51.730
The two bonded over daily lunches and late-night
strategizing — Churchill was known to work

00:15:51.730 --> 00:15:52.730
late into the night.

00:15:52.730 --> 00:15:53.730
By the time he left, F.D.R.

00:15:53.730 --> 00:15:57.220
found he missed his friend and sent a note
to Churchill in London, it read “It is fun

00:15:57.220 --> 00:15:59.259
to be in same decade with you.”

00:15:59.259 --> 00:16:03.180
With the Americans now in the war, and the
Russians fighting on the side of the Allies,

00:16:03.180 --> 00:16:08.439
Hitler’s armies were pushed back and victory
was declared on May 8, 1945 in Europe.

00:16:08.439 --> 00:16:12.610
Three months later in the east, Japan surrendered,
marking the end of World War II.

00:16:12.610 --> 00:16:15.709
In a surprise the same year, Churchill did
not win re-election.

00:16:15.709 --> 00:16:20.660
The End of an Era

00:16:20.660 --> 00:16:23.689
Following the war, Churchill continued to
influence world affairs.

00:16:23.689 --> 00:16:27.670
He gave his speech on the “iron curtain,”
about the USSR threat and was strongly in

00:16:27.670 --> 00:16:31.809
favor of Britain’s independence from the
European Coal and Steel Community, which was

00:16:31.809 --> 00:16:36.130
set up after the war to neutralize European
competition for natural resources.

00:16:36.130 --> 00:16:40.779
Churchill served as Prime Minister again in
1951 and resigned at the age of 80.

00:16:40.779 --> 00:16:43.639
By that time he was in deteriorating health.

00:16:43.639 --> 00:16:47.529
Churchill was intent on leaving his mark on
history, not just through clamoring for fame

00:16:47.529 --> 00:16:50.910
and power or placing himself at the center
of action.

00:16:50.910 --> 00:16:54.889
He would write his own story and solidify
a favorable view to be passed on through the

00:16:54.889 --> 00:16:55.889
ages.

00:16:55.889 --> 00:17:00.040
At the end of World War II, Churchill wrote
his best-selling series, The Second War World

00:17:00.040 --> 00:17:03.209
and won the Nobel Prize in Literature in 1953.

00:17:03.209 --> 00:17:06.579
In the same year he was knighted by Queen
Elizabeth II.

00:17:06.579 --> 00:17:11.270
In 1965, after suffering a stroke two weeks
earlier, Winston Churchill passed from this

00:17:11.270 --> 00:17:12.659
life into the next.

00:17:12.659 --> 00:17:17.860
It seemed the whole world felt the loss; and
reporters remarked it was the end of an era.

00:17:17.860 --> 00:17:22.900
The Queen saw to it that Churchill’s funeral
was the grandest of any commoner since 1852,

00:17:22.900 --> 00:17:26.589
“on a scale befitting his position in history.”

00:17:26.589 --> 00:17:32.929
His body lay in state in Westminster Hall
for three days and nights and no fewer than

00:17:32.929 --> 00:17:38.679
320,000 people filed past to pay their respects.

00:17:38.679 --> 00:17:43.539
Ten years ago, a fifth of all British teenagers
polled believed Churchill to be a fictional

00:17:43.539 --> 00:17:44.539
character.

00:17:44.539 --> 00:17:48.420
His legend may seem larger-than-life, and
knowing the man, apart from his greatest moment

00:17:48.420 --> 00:17:50.620
on the world’s stage, is harder to grasp.

00:17:50.620 --> 00:17:54.250
To say he was complex and contradictory is
an understatement.

00:17:54.250 --> 00:17:58.399
We know he held himself in the highest regard,
but Churchill also suffered in the depths

00:17:58.399 --> 00:18:02.200
of depression — he referred to it as his
“black dog.”

00:18:02.200 --> 00:18:06.350
His stubbornness, courage, grit, and brilliance
with words made him indispensable and his

00:18:06.350 --> 00:18:09.690
legacy is one that will live on and inspire
the good fight.

00:18:09.690 --> 00:18:14.059
And no doubt, Churchill will continue to be
judged and analyzed through the ages for his

00:18:14.059 --> 00:18:17.200
failed decisions and unpopular attitudes.

00:18:17.200 --> 00:18:20.059
He was a product of the time, with an impossible
job.

00:18:20.059 --> 00:18:23.880
“Statesmen are not called upon only to settle
easy questions.

00:18:23.880 --> 00:18:25.409
These often settle themselves.

00:18:25.409 --> 00:18:31.179
It is where the balance quivers, and the proportions
are veiled in mist, that the opportunity for

00:18:31.179 --> 00:19:06.730
world-saving decisions presents itself.”

